WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:02.669
In the world of boats

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.213
there are little boats
and bigger boats.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:07.841
And then there are the 
really big boats.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:09.551
This is a cruise ship.

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:11.177
The MSC Meraviglia

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:14.431
built in Saint-Nazaire, France, 
in service since 2017...

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:16.391
and christened by Sophia Loren.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.018
It is the—
[ship honks]

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:20.270
Sorry, she is the biggest
cruise ship

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.938
to have ever docked in New York City.

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:24.566
There's a water park, 
a rope course, a spa...

00:00:24.607 --> 00:00:27.277
a mall, an arcade, a bowling alley, 
a casino

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:28.570
a gym, two theaters

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.948
five pools, nine restaurants, 
23 bars, 19 floors

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:35.076
and enough room for 
5,655 passengers.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.746
and 1,536 crew members.

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:40.290
Cruise ships are the biggest passenger vessels

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.624
that humans have ever built.

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:45.003
They can fit a small town's worth 
of people into a single vehicle...

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:47.172
but they have a certain look to them.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.632
Far from the look of the big 
transatlantic ships

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:50.842
of 100 years ago.

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:55.055
So how did the biggest ships
we build wind up looking like this?

00:01:02.771 --> 00:01:05.023
Before cruise ships, the biggest
ships on earth

00:01:05.023 --> 00:01:06.066
were ocean liners.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:07.901
Built for one purpose:

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:09.694
To take you somewhere.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.156
In the golden days of ocean travel

00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:15.909
before the airplane, people 
had no choice.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.744
They had to use ships.

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:18.912
That's Peter Knego.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.247
He's a cruise journalist and ocean liner historian.

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:24.834
Advertisements from this 
golden era of ocean liners

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:26.836
boasted their speed across 
the Atlantic...

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:28.546
and their luxurious comfort...

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.882
which, given the conditions 
on the open ocean...

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:33.760
wasn't always easy to achieve.

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:37.222
If it's February and you're crossing
the Atlantic

00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:39.224
you're going to be absolutely miserable.

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:42.018
And the last thing you want to do is realize

00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:43.228
that you're on a ship.

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:48.233
What they used to do at the turn 
of the 20th century...

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:50.527
they would design at least the first class...

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.239
part of ships to look like 
great palatial hotels

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:56.241
or even palaces themselves.

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:59.369
Ocean liners like this one, 
the RMS Aquitania...

00:01:59.536 --> 00:02:02.288
tried to replicate all the amenities 
of life on land.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.083
There were restaurants, 
smoking rooms, gardens

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.335
and a massive lounge with
painted ceilings.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.705
For the first class passengers at least,

00:02:09.705 --> 00:02:10.964
all of the comforts of a city...

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:12.340
but at sea. 

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.635
There was a picture of the Aquitania 
lined up against

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:17.804
what was then the world's tallest building:

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:19.180
The Woolworth Building.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.851
And the Aquitania was longer and taller 
than the Woolworth Building.

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:25.311
And that was their way of saying 
this is like

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.772
literally a floating city at sea.

00:02:27.772 --> 00:02:31.234
Just to inspire the confidence 
of passengers.

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:36.573
But the rise of air travel meant that 
ships stopped being

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:38.283
being the only way to cross oceans.

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:39.534
And by the 1960s

00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:41.744
ocean liners were slowly becoming obsolete.

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:44.414
As ocean liner companies struggled 
to sell tickets...

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:45.790
they tried something different.

00:02:46.291 --> 00:02:49.085
“No longer do liners even attempt 
to compete with air speed.”

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:51.379
“Now the word is luxury.”

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:52.630
“A vacation at sea.”

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.008
They continued transatlantic service

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.759
in the northern Hemisphere’s
summer months.

00:02:56.801 --> 00:02:59.345
But in the winter months, 
more companies started offering

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.097
leisure trips to warmer regions...

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:04.350
and they started advertising 
ocean travel differently.

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:07.687
From selling transportation 
to selling a vacation.

00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:09.814
The cruise ship was born.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.192
But they got off to a slow start.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:16.154
They didn't really take off until 
the TV show The Love Boat.

00:03:18.781 --> 00:03:21.075
which exploded the idea 
of cruising.

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:24.329
It wasn't just something that 
your rich grandmother did

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:26.831
and made cruising something 
for the mainstream.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:29.918
But as cruises started to become mainstream

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:31.419
ocean liners had a problem.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:34.422
They were designed to go fast 
and consumed a lot of fuel.

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:38.009
They sat low in the water,
which kept them stable in rough seas...

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.762
but meant they could only access 
ports with deep harbors.

00:03:41.012 --> 00:03:43.890
They had separate sections for first, 
second, and third class...

00:03:44.265 --> 00:03:46.809
but that left a lot of passengers 
without access to amenities.

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:49.312
and limited everyone's freedom of movement
throughout the ship.

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:51.981
And compared to the full time 
cruise ships of the time

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:53.441
ocean liners were huge.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.611
So they'd have to attract a lot of vacationers
to be profitable.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.614
Everything that had made ocean liners 
optimally designed

00:03:59.614 --> 00:04:01.658
for commuting quickly 
through rough waters...

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.620
made them poorly fit for vacationing slowly 
through calm ones.

00:04:06.621 --> 00:04:08.331
As sea travel continued to dwindle

00:04:08.331 --> 00:04:11.501
even the largest ocean liner 
in the world, the SS France

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.628
couldn't generate enough revenue 
to operate.

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:17.465
And it sat idle for years until 1979

00:04:17.465 --> 00:04:19.801
when Norwegian Caribbean Lines
purchased it

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:22.095
and made an announcement that 
shocked the industry.

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.680
They were going to convert the France

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.431
into a full time cruise ship.

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:28.434
Since speed was no longer a concern

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:29.978
they shut down one engine room

00:04:29.978 --> 00:04:32.021
and removed two of the four propellers.

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:34.565
To get passengers to islands 
without deep ports

00:04:34.565 --> 00:04:35.692
they installed tenders.

00:04:35.692 --> 00:04:38.319
These are smaller ships that 
ferry passengers to shore.

00:04:38.736 --> 00:04:40.488
To open up amenities to all passengers

00:04:40.488 --> 00:04:42.657
they took out the barriers 
between class sections

00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:44.200
and to attract enough vacationers

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.369
they loaded the ship with 
a massive roster

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:47.578
of entertainment options.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.540
There are sporting facilities and 
shopping centers, cinema...

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.292
bars, a multi-confession church,
saunas

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:55.253
kindergarten, party games, educational courses, 
library, charades.

00:04:56.296 --> 00:05:00.008
The ship began service in 1980, 
renamed the SS Norway.

00:05:00.008 --> 00:05:02.093
Where other ships made 3 to 4 stops

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:03.303
in a weeklong cruise

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:04.846
The Norway only made two.

00:05:05.179 --> 00:05:07.932
They weren't advertising a cruise 
to a destination.

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:10.560
The ship was the destination itself.

00:05:11.769 --> 00:05:14.564
The Norway proved that size 
worked for cruise ships

00:05:14.564 --> 00:05:17.025
and it kicked off a race to build 
bigger and bigger ships...

00:05:17.275 --> 00:05:19.610
that changed the look of 
cruise ships forever.

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:22.655
To fit more cabins and amenities, 
those superstructures

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.825
that's all of this that rises above 
the deck, became taller...

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:28.077
which hid the once prominent smokestacks.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:31.622
The smokestacks, because the ships 
are so tall

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:34.208
the funnels are these tiny little afterthoughts.

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:36.419
To fit even more cabins 
and amenities

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:39.255
superstructures became wider, 
which shortened the bow.

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:41.007
That's this forward part of the ship.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:44.177
There is no space where there's just 
an open deck

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.637
where Jack and Rose can go stand

00:05:46.637 --> 00:05:48.556
and say they're king of the world 
or whatever.

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:49.724
That's all gone.

00:05:49.724 --> 00:05:52.060
The bow didn't need to be long 
and pointed to cut through

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.270
intense waves like on 
transatlantic liners.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.230
So builders rounded them out...

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:58.775
giving even more square footage 
for amenities.

00:05:59.942 --> 00:06:02.111
When the Norway began service in 1980

00:06:02.111 --> 00:06:04.906
it was the biggest operating 
passenger ship in the world.

00:06:05.156 --> 00:06:08.242
With an internal volume of over 
70,000 gross tons.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.411
But the ships built over 
the following decades

00:06:10.411 --> 00:06:12.205
make the Norway look tiny.

00:06:12.413 --> 00:06:15.208
And even make once legendary 
ocean liners look small.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:16.751
The ship that held the record for

00:06:16.751 --> 00:06:19.837
so many years was 
the Queen Elizabeth of 1940.

00:06:20.254 --> 00:06:21.881
She was 83,000 tons.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.967
People said they will never 
build a ship that big again.

00:06:25.259 --> 00:06:27.178
Well, now the new Royal Caribbean ships

00:06:27.178 --> 00:06:29.555
are literally three times the size.

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:33.518
When this ship, the Icon of the Seas,
launches in 2024...

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.144
it'll be all the way up here

00:06:35.144 --> 00:06:38.272
with a volume of over 250,000 gross tons.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:41.734
That elegance of design is missing

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.986
I think, on a lot of the big new ships.

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:45.488
It's just the way things go.

00:06:45.488 --> 00:06:49.409
And, you know, we all miss 
what came before and pine on.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:52.036
And I'm sure people in 50 
or 100 years from now

00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:53.955
will pine on about how great-looking

00:06:53.955 --> 00:06:56.582
the ships were back in the 2020s.

00:06:57.625 --> 00:06:59.085
There are over 320

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:01.087
cruise ships sailing around the world 
right now...

00:07:01.504 --> 00:07:03.339
but there's only one ocean liner left.

00:07:04.006 --> 00:07:07.760
The Queen Mary 2, also built 
in Saint-Nazaire, France

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.181
in service since 2004 and 
christened by Elizabeth II. 

00:07:12.473 --> 00:07:15.643
Still in regular service from Southampton 
to New York City.

